#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Launch Firefox inside its own memory-capped cgroup.
#
# WHY A WRAPPER AND NOT A PREF: Firefox has no "use at most N GB" setting. Every
# pref-level knob (bfcache size, undo history, process count) trims the baseline
# by a few hundred MB and then stops mattering, because the real cost is the
# pages themselves - a dozen permanently-open SPAs (Teams, WhatsApp, Gmail,
# Docs, Claude) that each legitimately want 300-600 MB. You cannot pref your way
# out of that. You can only put a ceiling on it from outside.
#
# WHY A WRAPPER AND NOT A DROP-IN: this used to be
# ~/.config/systemd/user/app-org.mozilla.firefox@.service.d/50-memory.conf.
# That worked because PLASMA launched .desktop apps as transient systemd user
# units named app-<desktop-id>@<hex>.service, so Firefox already had its own
# cgroup and a drop-in on the template unit applied to every instance.
#
# niri does not do that. Its shipped unit sets Slice=session.slice and apps it
# spawns are direct children of the compositor, so no app-*.service is ever
# created and the drop-in matches nothing - silently. No error, no log line,
# just an uncapped browser. Measured on the old machine, that is a 8.13 GiB
# working set with nothing holding it back.
#
# `systemd-run --user --scope` is the same primitive the `capped` helper in
# .zshrc already uses. --slice=app.slice also moves Firefox out of niri's own
# cgroup, so systemd-oomd can act on the browser rather than the whole session.
#
# MemoryHigh, not MemoryMax:
#   MemoryHigh = throttle + reclaim. Over the line the kernel pushes Firefox's
#                coldest pages out until it fits. Nothing dies.
#   MemoryMax  = hard wall. Over the line the cgroup OOM killer shoots Firefox.
# We want the first. zram is the swap device here (zstd, ~3.79:1 measured), so
# "reclaimed" means "compressed in RAM at about a third of the size", not "read
# back off the NVMe". That is why this is cheap on this machine specifically,
# and it is the same reasoning behind vm.swappiness = 180.
#
# 6G of 15.3G total leaves ~9G for Docker, Nx/Angular, .NET and the desktop.
#
# CAVEAT, so this is not mistaken for something it is not: this makes the KERNEL
# reclaim Firefox's pages. It does NOT make Firefox release anything. Firefox's
# own browser.tabs.unloadOnLowMemory watcher polls system-wide MemAvailable and
# is not cgroup-aware, so it will not fire because of this cap.
#
# SUPERSEDED BY uwsm: once the session is started with `uwsm start`, apps get
# real per-app units again (app-niri-firefox@<hex>.service) and the original
# drop-in approach comes back - see docs/SETUP-GUIDE.md. Use one or the other,
# never both: under uwsm this wrapper would produce a unit named
# app-niri-firefox-capped@... which a firefox drop-in would not match.
exec systemd-run --user --scope --collect --quiet \
  --slice=app.slice \
  --unit="firefox-$$" \
  -p MemoryHigh=6G \
  -p MemoryAccounting=yes \
  -- /usr/bin/firefox "$@"
